# RESURGAM Recovery Lab agent configuration (ADR 0019).
# Installed as /etc/default/resurgam-lab-agent -- a dpkg conffile, so
# your edits survive package upgrades. Restart the service after
# changing anything here: systemctl restart resurgam-lab-agent

# Pairing a node from RESURGAM's Lab Compute wizard (ADR 0027) writes
# this file for you. Edit it by hand only for a manual install or to
# add the PBS token later.

# Required -- the agent refuses to start without these two:

# The RESURGAM backend's API base URL.
#RESURGAM_BACKEND_URL=https://resurgam.example.com/api/v1

# This cluster's agent token. Set automatically by pairing; for a manual
# install generate/rotate it in RESURGAM under Infrastructure -> PVE
# Targets -> this cluster -> "resurgam-lab-agent token" ("Generate /
# rotate token"). Shown exactly once at generation time.
#RESURGAM_LAB_AGENT_TOKEN=

# Optional since 0.2.6 -- this agent's own local PBS API token,
# "<token-id>:<secret>" (e.g. "resurgam@pbs!dev:xxxxxxxx-xxxx-..."), with
# at least Datastore.Read on whatever datastore(s) this cluster's
# workloads are backed up to. Never sent to RESURGAM -- used only to
# authenticate this host's own `proxmox-backup-client` calls.
# Without it the agent still starts, but only runs template builds,
# appliance snippet jobs and the guest-exec "live" indexing hook; lab
# runs, ephemeral file indexing and Disk Mounts wait until it is set.
#RESURGAM_LAB_AGENT_PBS_TOKEN=

# The appliance's TLS CA certificate (written by pairing, so the agent
# trusts the appliance's internal HTTPS certificate). Empty = the
# system trust store (use that when RESURGAM has a publicly trusted
# certificate).
#RESURGAM_LAB_AGENT_CA_FILE=

# Optional -- defaults shown:
#RESURGAM_LAB_AGENT_POLL_INTERVAL_SECONDS=5
# PVE "dir" storage (content "images") whose path is OVERLAY_DIR -- must
# exist on this node; RESURGAM's "Set up lab compute" wizard can create it.
#RESURGAM_LAB_AGENT_OVERLAY_STORAGE_ID=resurgam-lab-overlays
#RESURGAM_LAB_AGENT_OVERLAY_DIR=/var/lib/resurgam-lab-agent/overlays
#RESURGAM_LAB_AGENT_VM_MEMORY_MB=2048
#RESURGAM_LAB_AGENT_VM_CORES=2
#RESURGAM_LAB_AGENT_GUEST_EXEC_AGENT_WAIT_TIMEOUT_SECONDS=600
# This node's PVE node name (default: the short host name, which is what
# PVE uses). Disk Mounts (ADR 0025) refuse a job meant for another node.
# Set it only if the PVE node name differs from the host name.
#RESURGAM_LAB_AGENT_NODE_NAME=
# How long a share helper VM may take to boot and export the disks.
#RESURGAM_LAB_AGENT_DISK_MOUNT_READY_TIMEOUT_SECONDS=600

# Guest-exec File Indexing's "live" method (ADR 0021) needs ONE manual,
# one-time step this package does not do for you: add a line to
# /etc/vzdump.conf (create it if it doesn't exist) so vzdump actually
# invokes the shipped hookscript on every real backup --
#
#   script: /usr/share/resurgam-lab-agent/hooks/resurgam-guest-exec-live-hook.sh
#
# This is deliberately NOT the per-VM `hookscript` property (`qm set
# <vmid> --hookscript ...`) -- confirmed live (2026-09-24) that PVE's
# per-VM hookscript only fires on VM start/stop, never on a backup; the
# mechanism that actually fires on backup phases is vzdump's own,
# separate, node-level `script:` option. The shipped script itself
# re-checks with the RESURGAM backend whether a given VM's backup is
# actually relevant before doing anything, so this one line applies
# correctly node-wide -- no per-VM PVE config to maintain.

# GUEST-EXEC FILE INDEXING IS NOT RECOMMENDED AS A PRIMARY METHOD RIGHT
# NOW (ADR 0021). Every stage up to final retrieval works and is live-
# verified, but retrieval itself has failed deterministically in every
# one of four independent live test rounds against a real production
# VM, and the root cause was never isolated despite substantial
# investigation (a size-ceiling theory and a content-triggered-AV-
# scanning theory were both tested directly and disproven/ineffective).
# If you still want to try it: as a general precaution (not a confirmed
# fix for the above), exclude RESURGAM's working directory from the
# guest's antivirus -- every mainstream backup agent requires the same
# class of exclusion for its own working path. For the built-in
# Windows Defender, run inside each opted-in Windows guest (elevated
# PowerShell):
#
#   Add-MpPreference -ExclusionPath 'C:\Windows\Temp\resurgam-index-chunk-*'
#   Add-MpPreference -ExclusionPath 'C:\Windows\Temp\resurgam-index-manifest.txt'
#
# In an environment where local Defender settings are centrally managed
# (Microsoft Defender for Endpoint / Group Policy), add the equivalent
# managed exclusion instead -- a locally-run `Add-MpPreference` won't
# stick if policy overrides it. See ADR 0021's live-verification
# addendum for the full, honest history of what was tried.
